  "minutes": 22,
  "title": {
    "en": "Female diving ducks"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Tufted, Scaup, Pochard, Ferruginous, Red-crested Pochard, Ring-necked Duck and Goldeneye"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "The diving ducks are rounder, lower in the water and more compact than the dabbling ducks. The females are brown and grey, and the same rules apply: head shape, bill pattern and the pale marks on the face. The most useful clue for a diving duck is the shape of the head: peaked, rounded, or flat.\n\nLakes, reservoirs and gravel pits are the best habitats from October to March. In hard winters, Tufted Ducks, Pochards, Scaup and Goldeneyes arrive from the Baltic and from Russia, and pits on the coast fill with them. The rarer ones, like the Ring-necked Duck and Ferruginous Duck, hide in a flock of the common ones. The females are what you look at.\n\nAs in lesson 21, every photo here is a female, and the wrong answers in the quiz are other females."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "nail"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The hard tip at the end of a duck's bill. Its size and colour differ: a Tufted Duck has a broad black nail."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "peaked crown"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A head shape where the highest point is at the back of the crown, as in the Ring-necked Duck."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "face patch"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A pale patch on the face at the base of the bill. It is wide and white on a Scaup."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "eye ring"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A pale ring around the eye. It is bold on a female Ring-necked Duck."
      }
    }
  ],
